1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:12,880
This is a short walkthrough of a proposed virtual lab for Experiment 10 of the MBBS biochemistry journal: qualitative analysis of abnormal urine.

2
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,536
The idea is simple.

3
00:00:14,536 --> 00:00:20,023
Every test is simulated from the chemistry of the urine sample.

4
00:00:20,023 --> 00:00:28,641
So what a student sees on the laptop can be checked, tube by tube, against the real wet lab.

5
00:00:28,891 --> 00:00:30,827
Here is how it works.

6
00:00:30,827 --> 00:00:40,968
The urine sample is defined by what it contains: albumin, red cells, glucose, ketone bodies, bile, and its pH.

7
00:00:40,968 --> 00:00:51,202
Each test is then simulated from its chemistry: reaction rates, temperature, pH, diffusion and surface tension.

8
00:00:51,202 --> 00:00:56,641
The colours are computed from how the liquid absorbs light.

9
00:00:56,641 --> 00:01:06,229
The same sample is prepared in the wet lab from a recipe, tested by the journal procedure, and compared.

10
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:12,361
If a bench differs, one measurement re-calibrates the model.

11
00:01:12,611 --> 00:01:17,185
The lab opens with case report one from the journal.

12
00:01:17,185 --> 00:01:27,037
John, eight years old, with lethargy, puffiness of the face, reddish urine, and a blood pressure of 140/90 mmHg.

13
00:01:27,037 --> 00:01:30,116
The first decision is the specimen.

14
00:01:30,116 --> 00:01:41,800
For an 8-year-old who can co-operate, a midstream clean-catch sample is appropriate, and the lab gives feedback on the choice.

15
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:48,053
In the physical examination, the colour of the urine is not a picture.

16
00:01:48,053 --> 00:01:54,055
It is computed from the light absorption of urochrome and haemoglobin.

17
00:01:54,055 --> 00:02:04,603
The student records volume, colour and appearance, wafts the odour, and reads the pH paper against its colour chart.

18
00:02:04,853 --> 00:02:11,213
Specific gravity is read on a virtual urinometer at the lower meniscus.

19
00:02:11,213 --> 00:02:17,662
The room temperature is set to the real thermometer reading, here 28 °C.

20
00:02:17,662 --> 00:02:26,709
The student applies the journal's correction: 0.001 for every 3 °C above the calibration temperature.

21
00:02:26,709 --> 00:02:32,773
The lab then checks every entry against the simulated result.

22
00:02:33,023 --> 00:02:46,911
In the chemical tests, the student first selects the right test for each abnormal constituent, exactly as the case report asks.

23
00:02:47,161 --> 00:02:51,669
Each test follows the journal procedure step by step.

24
00:02:51,669 --> 00:03:01,025
In the heat coagulation test, only the upper part of the tube is heated, so the lower part stays as a control.

25
00:03:01,025 --> 00:03:13,444
The upper portion turns cloudy, and after acetic acid the turbidity persists, because the denatured albumin aggregates near its isoelectric point.

26
00:03:13,444 --> 00:03:19,828
The model calculates this from the albumin concentration and the pH.

27
00:03:20,078 --> 00:03:33,540
Benedict's test stays blue for John, because there is no reducing sugar in his urine.

28
00:03:33,790 --> 00:03:38,813
The benzidine test turns blue-green almost immediately.

29
00:03:38,813 --> 00:03:48,767
In the model this is haem peroxidase kinetics: 10 000 RBC/µL give a colour that is visible in under a second.

30
00:03:48,767 --> 00:03:56,673
So John has protein and blood in the urine, which fits acute glomerulonephritis.

31
00:03:56,923 --> 00:04:01,209
The dipstick is timed, as the journal requires.

32
00:04:01,209 --> 00:04:09,325
The pads develop colour over time from their indicator chemistry, shown here at 6× speed.

33
00:04:09,325 --> 00:04:27,114
Readings must be taken between 40 s and 2 min, and the lab flags readings taken too early or too late.

34
00:04:27,364 --> 00:04:34,851
Case report two is the 50-year-old diabetic woman in coma, with Kussmaul breathing.

35
00:04:34,851 --> 00:04:47,659
Benedict's test, boiled for 2 min, goes from blue through green and orange to a brick red precipitate, because the urine contains 3 % glucose.

36
00:04:47,659 --> 00:04:53,974
Rothera's test forms a purple ring at the junction, from acetoacetate.

37
00:04:53,974 --> 00:05:06,841
The model also knows that β-hydroxybutyrate, the main ketone in ketoacidosis, does not react, so urine tests can under-estimate ketosis.

38
00:05:07,092 --> 00:05:13,312
The reaction simulator shows why each step of the procedure matters.

39
00:05:13,312 --> 00:05:21,911
With 2 % glucose, boiling on a flame for 2 min reduces most of the copper and gives brick red.

40
00:05:21,911 --> 00:05:27,217
In a 60 °C water bath, the same sample only reaches green.

41
00:05:27,217 --> 00:05:31,210
Under-heating under-reads glycosuria.

42
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:39,681
For heat coagulation, the simulator shows the effect of acetic acid on pH and on aggregation.

43
00:05:39,681 --> 00:05:48,503
For Rothera's test, it shows ammonia diffusing down the tube, which is what creates the ring.

44
00:05:48,753 --> 00:05:53,004
This is how we propose to compare with the wet lab.

45
00:05:53,004 --> 00:06:13,595
For each test the model predicts something measurable: the mass of copper oxide in Benedict's test, the absorbance after sulphosalicylic acid, the seconds until the Rothera ring or the benzidine colour appears, and photographs of the tube colours.

46
00:06:13,595 --> 00:06:17,346
Entering a bench value gives agree or differ.

47
00:06:17,346 --> 00:06:21,098
The value typed here is only an illustration.

48
00:06:21,098 --> 00:06:27,284
A printable standards series checks the model over its whole range.

49
00:06:27,534 --> 00:06:44,490
For the wet lab, the faculty tab gives a recipe for each case, per 100 mL of pooled urine: albumin and fresh blood for case one, glucose and acetoacetate for case two, with the expected answer key.

50
00:06:44,490 --> 00:06:53,611
A custom builder lets faculty design unknown samples and see the predicted result before the class.

51
00:06:53,862 --> 00:07:04,057
One part of the chemistry was cross-checked with the open source program PHREEQC, from the US Geological Survey.

52
00:07:04,057 --> 00:07:09,883
It showed that my first model of the acetic acid step was wrong.

53
00:07:09,883 --> 00:07:14,616
The corrected model now agrees within 0.02 pH units.

54
00:07:14,616 --> 00:07:25,776
There is no open source simulator for qualitative urinalysis, so the kinetics and colours are this lab's own models.

55
00:07:26,026 --> 00:07:30,445
What I propose is a short pilot with your department.

56
00:07:30,445 --> 00:07:43,453
We prepare the two case samples from the recipe, run the journal procedure in the wet lab and on the laptop side by side, and record agreement test by test.

57
00:07:43,453 --> 00:07:49,040
Your clinical review of the cases and interpretations is essential.

58
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:58,629
The constants come from published values, so agreement with our own bench is exactly what the pilot will establish.

59
00:07:58,629 --> 00:08:00,063
Thank you.
